\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gregory Schmidt and The Associated Press \par \uc2 517 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 AUTO\par \par \uc2 Ford to Cut 4,000 Jobs\par \par \uc2 In Europe by 2027\par \par \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  said Wednesday that it would eliminate 4,000 jobs in Europe as the automaker struggled with slowing demand for electric vehicles and increased competition from Chinese companies.\par \par \uc2 The company said the jobs cuts, which will take place by the end of 2027, were part of a broader plan to strengthen its business in Europe. The reductions will primarily affect plants in Germany and Britain.\par \par \uc2 Ford added that it would reduce production of an electric version of its Explorer sport utility vehicle, which would result in shorter working hours at a plant in Cologne, Germany, in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The company said it was committed to building a thriving business in Europe. ''It is critical to take difficult but decisive action to ensure Ford's future competitiveness in Europe,'' Dave Johnston, Ford's European vice president for transformation and partnerships, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Ford is pulling back as the auto industry tries to navigate the bumpy transition to electric vehicles. The growth rate of E.V. sales has slowed this year in some European countries. Ford has also struggled to sell as many battery-powered models as it had hoped in the United States and said in August that it would reduce the pace of investments in such vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Other automakers, including \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  and \uc2 Mercedes-Benz\uc2 , have also shifted their strategies after reporting fewer sales of battery-powered vehicles than they had expected. GREGORY SCHMIDT\par \par \uc2 FOOD\par \par \uc2 Campbell's Deletes Soup\par \par \uc2 From Corporate Name\par \par \uc2 Campbell's is ditching the soup -- at least in name. And the change has now received shareholders' greenlight.\par \par \uc2 Campbell Soup Co.\uc2  announced its intention to change its name just over two months ago, saying it wanted to instead be known as the (slightly shorter) \uc2 Campbell's Co.\uc2  Shareholders overwhelmingly approved the change on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, CEO \uc2 Mark Clouse\uc2  maintained that this ''subtle yet important change'' will retain the company's iconic name ''while reflecting the full breadth of our portfolio.''\par \par \uc2 New Jersey-based Campbell's hasn't been exclusive to the soup business for some time. The company also owns brands like Prego sauce and Goldfish crackers -- and completed a $2.7 billion acquisition of \uc2 Sovos Brands\uc2 , the maker of Rao's pasta sauces, earlier this year.\par \par \uc2 Still, the 155-year-old food seller is most famous for its namesake canned soups, adorned with red and white labels. Its new name won't change that, the company says -- noting the Campbell's soup brand or packaging will not be affected.\par \par \uc2 ''We love soup and always will,'' Clouse stated Tuesday. ''The iconic red and white Campbell's soup labels that consumers love will be on shelves forever.''\par \par \uc2 Campbell's is far from the first to attempt such as makeover. From Dunkin' to \uc2 KFC\uc2 , other food companies have rebranded themselves -- often shedding the mention of a specific item they were known for with a shorter corporate title, or even an updated logo -- in hopes of emphasizing other business. ASSOCIATED PRESS\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241121ekbl0004z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
