\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Required Anti-Union Meetings Are Banned by the N.L.R.B.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danielle Kaye \par \uc2 631 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The ruling, stemming from a complaint against Amazon, bars companies from compelling workers to attend meetings on unionization's downsides.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  ruled on Wednesday that companies may not compel workers to attend meetings on the downsides of unionization, a tactic that unions say stifles worker organizing.\par \par \uc2 The decision, the latest in a slew of labor board rulings under the Biden administration aimed at supporting workers' right to unionize, stems from a complaint over Amazon's conduct before a successful union election in 2022 at a Staten Island warehouse, the first Amazon warehouse in the nation to unionize. The company held hundreds of meetings there and at another location to discourage workers from supporting a union.\par \par \uc2 The N.L.R.B.'s ban on so-called captive audience meetings is a precedent with potential impact beyond Amazon, though it could be reversed after President-elect Donald J. Trump takes office. Facing a wave of union campaigns since the onset of the pandemic, large employers including \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Trader Joe's and REI have held such meetings in what labor regulators and unions have described as an effort to clamp down on organizing. The companies have denied accusations of anti-union campaigns.\par \par \uc2 These meetings, which employees are often required to attend, give employers ''near-unfettered freedom to force their message about unionization on workers,'' \uc2 Lauren McFerran\uc2 , the Democratic chairman of the labor board, said in a statement. She added that they undermine employees' ability to choose whether they want union representation, a right guaranteed under federal law.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision better protects workers' freedom to make their own choices in exercising their rights,'' Ms. McFerran said, ''while ensuring that employers can convey their views about unionization in a noncoercive manner.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon intends to appeal the decision, said Mary Kate Paradis, a company spokeswoman, calling the ruling a violation of the First Amendment and adding that it ''contradicts the express language'' of the \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act. Meetings are often held ''because the decision about whether or not to join a union is an important one, and employees deserve to understand the facts so they can make an informed choice,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The N.L.R.B. voted 3 to 1 to issue the ban, which overrules a decades-old standard that paved the way for mandatory meetings about unionization to become widespread. The Democratic majority voted in favor of the decision, while the board's one Republican member dissented.\par \par \uc2 Deeming captive audience meetings unlawful is ''a long overdue win for workers,'' said Kara Deniz, a spokeswoman for the \uc2 Teamsters union\uc2 , which is pushing to unionize workers across Amazon's business, from warehouses to delivery routes. Amazon ''can look at this ruling as an opportunity to redirect the tens of millions it spends on union busters each year back into the rightful pockets of workers,'' Ms. Deniz said.\par \par \uc2 Whether the labor board ruling will survive under Mr. Trump remains unclear.\par \par \uc2 Labor experts predict a sharp shift away from the largely pro-union stance taken by the Biden administration's N.L.R.B., potentially reversing some decisions. Notably, Mr. Trump could oust \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2 , the general counsel, who has taken an aggressive approach to wielding the agency's authority on behalf of workers seeking to unionize.\par \par \uc2 ''She's been a general counsel who has taken the position seriously in enforcing the National Labor Relations Act to its fullest extent,'' said \uc2 Risa Lieberwitz\uc2 , a professor of labor and employment law at \uc2 Cornell University\uc2 . ''It certainly is likely that once Trump is in office, he will remove her, and appoint someone who is more akin to Trump's politics on labor.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241114ekbe0005p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
