\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Media; Times Tech Guild Ends Strike With No Contract\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Robertson \par \uc2 261 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The union said its members would return to work on Tuesday, even though it had not yet reached a contract deal with the company.\par \par \uc2 The Times Tech Guild, which represents more than 600 tech workers at \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , announced on Monday that it had ended its weeklong strike despite not reaching a deal on a contract.\par \par \uc2 The union has been on strike since Nov. 4 and has regularly picketed outside The Times's Manhattan headquarters. The strike was planned to coincide with Election Day, when readership interest is high.\par \par \uc2 The Times Tech Guild workers, which include software developers, designers and data analysts, will return to their jobs on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Zhang, the unit chair of the union and a senior analytics manager at The Times, said the strike ''showed that we have the full support of subscribers and allies across the country going forward.'' She said the union would continue to fight for a fair contract.\par \par \uc2 ''We look forward to continuing to work with Tech Guild to reach a fair contract that takes into account that they are already among the highest paid individual contributors in the company and journalism is our top priority,'' said \uc2 Danielle Rhoades Ha\uc2 , a spokeswoman for The Times.\par \par \uc2 The sticking points in the contract negotiations have included pay increases and pay equity, and whether the workers could have a ''just cause'' provision in their contract, which means they can be terminated only for misconduct or another such reason.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241112ekbc0004b\par }\page {\p