\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Big Tech Employees Quiet About Election This Time\b0\par\par\uc2 By Karen Weise, Nico Grant and Mike Isaac \par \uc2 940 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Eight years ago, workers loudly protested White House policies. This time around, the companies are trying to keep a lid on activism.\par \par \uc2 If any industry could have said its workplaces were politicized, it was tech.\par \par \uc2 Early in \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's first term in the White House, America's tech giants loudly protested his temporary ban on travel from seven predominantly Muslim countries.\par \par \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 's chief executive, posted that his great-grandparents were immigrants and that his in-laws were refugees. \uc2 Sergey Brin\uc2 , a co-founder of \uc2 Google\uc2  who immigrated from the Soviet Union as a child, rushed to San Francisco International Airport to protest.\par \par \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 's chief executive, joined a crowd of employees rallying at the company's corporate headquarters.\par \par \uc2 ''Wow, thank you everyone for showing up today,'' he said to cheers. ''It's remarkable.''\par \par \uc2 But after this week's presidential election, the largely liberal work forces of tech's biggest companies were quiet. While the definitive nature of the election most likely played a role, the change also represented an effort by executives to dampen employee activism in recent years. They put in place policies restricting dialogue, monitored internal chat channels and vowed not to weigh in on the issues that fired up activist employees.\par \par \uc2 If the presidential election was going to be the biggest test of these new rules, the vigilance wasn't necessary. Reactions to the election on company message boards and forums was muted.\par \par \uc2 On a companywide \uc2 Google\uc2  forum, someone posted an American flag with the note ''Sending support to American colleagues,'' which was liked by more than 1,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 ''My heart is heavy,'' one \uc2 Amazon\uc2  employee wrote in an internal chat. ''I may not know you, but know that I love you.''\par \par \uc2 The heads of most of the country's leading tech employers didn't acknowledge the election results to their staffs, though many of them posted their congratulations to Mr. Trump on X, praising his ''decisive'' and ''hard-fought'' victory.\par \par \uc2 "Congratulations to President @realDonaldTrump on his decisive victory,'' Mr. Pichai wrote on X. ''We are in a golden age of American innovation and are committed to working with his administration to help bring the benefits to everyone.''\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's victory landed in an industry deeply changed since his last term. Tech companies had long espoused the idea of bringing your whole self to work, and they were built on a culture of blunt expression. But the turmoil of the past several years -- from the \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protests in 2020 to the war in Gaza -- have turned executives off from the freewheeling cultures they once fostered.\par \par \uc2 And as they have focused their companies on efficiency coming out of the pandemic, they have also taken a more assertive approach to their own workers, enacting in some cases the largest layoffs in their histories and demanding that people return to working in an office -- or show themselves the door.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  since 2019 has instructed employees to keep politics out of the workplace, and the company cracked down on its irreverent Memegen forum, its corporate town square, this year after workers feuded about the war in Gaza.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Mr. Pichai reminded his 181,000 employees to not discuss politics in the workplace regardless of who won the election, according to an email reviewed by \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Whomever the voters entrust, let's remember the role we play at work, through the products we build and as a business: to be a trusted source of information to people of every background and belief,'' Mr. Pichai wrote. The company is ''at its best'' when focusing on its mission and products, he added.\par \par \uc2 Employees largely had conversations in smaller groups and more private forums. A few looked for ways to express their discontent without drawing the attention of the company's internal content moderators. The moderators were removing political posts and temporarily suspending uploading privileges for employees who repeatedly broke the rules, said three people with knowledge of the moderation, who were not authorized to publicly discuss it. CNBC earlier reported on the takedowns and Mr. Pichai's email.\par \par \uc2 Moderators also took down vague posts without captions that were assumed to be about the election, one of the people said, including a number of posts featuring black squares, a symbol of mourning.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2 's human resources department, with support from Mr. Zuckerberg, introduced a policy in late 2022 called ''community engagement expectations,'' according to a copy of the memo reviewed by The Times. It expressly forbade employees to discuss in the workplace hot-button political issues, including abortion, racial justice movements, wars and political news.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's senior leaders pulled back this year on issuing frequent statements on political or cultural issues, according to three people familiar with the decision who spoke on the condition of anonymity.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, several \uc2 Amazon\uc2  executives spoke about the future in a two-hour companywide meeting and did not mention the election once. \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , the company's chief executive, defended the company's office mandate against claims it was a ''backdoor layoff,'' expressed hope in the company' A.I. innovations and praised experiments to deliver eggs faster to customers.\par \par \uc2 But the real test of tech's newfound neutrality will be once Mr. Trump takes office, if he follows through on his promises on issues the companies have opposed.\par \par \uc2 ''Remember the famous travel ban?'' he said at a September rally. He vowed to bring it back.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241111ekbb0003s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
