\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Strike Is Over, But Its Troubles Are Not\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 918 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The aerospace manufacturer and its new chief executive face a daunting to-do list, including improving quality and increasing production of its commercial planes.\par \par \uc2 After nearly two months on strike, some 33,000 \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employees will return to work over the next week. But getting the troubled manufacturer back on track will take a lot longer.\par \par \uc2 Even before the strike began on Sept. 13, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  faced challenges, including a quality crisis, mounting debt and supply chain chaos. The Monday vote by union members to accept a contract and end the strike will put the focus back on how the company and its new chief executive, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , plan to address those festering problems.\par \par \uc2 ''Resolution of the strike was low-hanging fruit,'' Jonathan Root, a senior vice president for \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Ratings, said in a statement on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at some of the items at the top of Mr. Ortberg's to-do list.\par \par \uc2 Improve Quality\par \par \uc2 Mr. Ortberg joined \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in August, inheriting a company in trouble.\par \par \uc2 In January, a panel blew off a 737 Max plane during an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight, resurfacing concerns about the quality and safety of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes five years after two fatal Max crashes. The \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  increased oversight of the company and limited production of the jet.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is working to improve quality, including adding more inspections internally and at its suppliers. The company is giving employees more training, simplifying plans and procedures, and reducing the amount of work performed out of sequence to minimize the risk of mistakes.\par \par \uc2 But that work will take time to bear fruit, and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  will need to show the F.A.A. that it is making progress if it wants the regulator's permission to increase production beyond a limit imposed after the January incident.\par \par \uc2 Make More Jets\par \par \uc2 The strike has been particularly damaging for \uc2 Boeing\uc2  because most of the 33,000 union members help make most of its commercial airplanes. The walkout brought that work to a standstill, taking a financial toll on the company, the region and the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The strike cost \uc2 Boeing\uc2  about $6.5 billion in lost earnings through October, according to the Anderson Economic Group, a research and consulting firm in Michigan. Those losses total about $11.5 billion when including costs to workers, suppliers, customers and the Seattle region.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was trying to increase production of the Max, its most popular and lucrative commercial plane, before the strike. Restarting production will be slow. Workers have until next Tuesday to get back to work. Some will require retraining, and it could be weeks before operations are running smoothly.\par \par \uc2 ''Historically, post-strike productivity takes a couple months to recover, and given the heightened focus on quality and safety, we are not expecting anything shorter,'' Myles Walton, an analyst at \uc2 Wolfe Research\uc2 , said in a research note.\par \par \uc2 Stabilize the Business\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has also faced mounting debt and rising costs in recent years.\par \par \uc2 In October, Mr. Ortberg announced plans to restructure the company, including by cutting about 17,000 jobs, or 10 percent of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's global work force. Last week, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  raised more than $21 billion by selling shares to investors to strengthen its financial position and stave off the loss of its investment-grade credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  still has to complete work on fixed-price government contracts that have become a financial strain because the company's costs have increased more than expected. It needs to work with the F.A.A. to get a handful of delayed commercial airplanes certified. And Mr. Ortberg has also said he is looking to streamline \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's sprawling operation, which could mean jettisoning some businesses, including perhaps some of its space programs.\par \par \uc2 ''It's something a new C.E.O. always does when you come into a business,'' he told investors last month. ''What do we want this company to look like five and 10 years from now? Do these things add value to the company or distract us? So, I'm in the process of going through that.''\par \par \uc2 Catch Up to \uc2 Airbus\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was the dominant manufacturer of commercial airplanes for decades. But that changed a few years ago. More passenger planes in use today were made by the company's main rival, \uc2 Airbus\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Regaining the ground \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has lost will not be easy. And doing so will probably rest on whatever jet the company makes next, a program Mr. Ortberg is expected to oversee.\par \par \uc2 If plans for that next plane come together in the next four years, the company will have to carry out that work in the Seattle area, under its new labor contract. That was an important concession to the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 . Many of the union's members are still angry that the company years ago moved production of the 787 Dreamliner to a factory in South Carolina where workers are not unionized.\par \par \uc2 ''They've never given us a commitment for an airplane program before it's launched, before it's designed,'' Jon Holden, the president of District 751 of the union, which represents most of the workers covered by the contract, said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 But rolling out a new plane is a complex process. Manufacturers produce versions of the same jet for decades. That means they have to plan way ahead, including making sure airlines will keep buying the model for a long time.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241106ekb60003j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
