\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Striking Boeing Workers to Vote Monday on Company's Third Contract Offer\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 977 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The aerospace manufacturer's largest union said it would put the contract to a vote on Monday by its 33,000 members, who rejected two earlier agreements.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest union said on Thursday that it would hold a vote on a new contract offer, after workers rejected two earlier proposals. The union's 33,000 members have been on strike since Sept. 13, dealing a damaging blow to the struggling aerospace manufacturer.\par \par \uc2 The offer was negotiated by company and union leaders, with help from Biden administration officials, including the acting labor secretary, Julie Su. In a statement, the union encouraged workers to accept the offer in voting scheduled for Monday.\par \par \uc2 ''It is time for our members to lock in these gains and confidently declare victory,'' said a statement from the leaders of two chapters of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 , who represent the workers on strike. ''We believe asking members to stay on strike longer wouldn't be right as we have achieved so much success.''\par \par \uc2 If workers do not take the deal, they ''risk a regressive or lesser offer in the future,'' the union leaders warned. District 751 of the union represents the vast majority of the workers, while another chapter, District W24, represents the rest.\par \par \uc2 The workers mostly support the company's commercial airplane division in the Seattle area, where \uc2 Boeing\uc2  builds most such jets. They walked off the job after 95 percent of those voting rejected a contract that union and company leaders had negotiated. The workers rejected a second offer with better terms last week, with 64 percent voting against the proposal. The union has not said how many people participated in either vote.\par \par \uc2 The new contract offer represents a slight improvement over the recently rejected proposal. It would raise wages cumulatively by more than 43 percent over the four years of the contract, up from nearly 40 percent in the last offer, according to details shared by the union. The deal also includes a $12,000 bonus for agreeing to the contract, which can be diverted in any amount to employee retirement plans. That figure combines a $7,000 ratification bonus and a $5,000 one-time retirement contribution in the previous offer.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said the average machinist would see annual pay rise to more than $119,000 by the end of the contract, up from nearly $76,000 today.\par \par \uc2 Many workers are still furious over the loss of a defined-benefit pension a decade ago. That anger fueled resistance to the previous offers, even among those who don't expect the company to restore the pension. In interviews before the vote last week, many \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers said they felt they deserved more out of a contract and were confident that they would get that by continuing to strike.\par \par \uc2 If members approve the contract, it will replace a 2008 agreement that was reached after a two-month strike. That walkout contributed to a decline in revenue of about $6.4 billion that year because the company delivered 104 fewer planes than expected.\par \par \uc2 A rejection would extend the strike, which has taken a substantial toll on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , costing the company at least $5.5 billion in lost earnings in its first six weeks, according to an analysis by Anderson Economic Group, a research and consulting firm in Michigan. The total economic fallout of the strike through October, including its effect on suppliers and customers, was more than $9.6 billion.\par \par \uc2 The strike has also jeopardized \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's investment-grade credit rating. Depending on how long the walkout lasts, the company could face a downgrade to junk status, which could raise its borrowing costs, ratings agencies have warned.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has taken several cost-cutting measures, pausing some spending and issuing temporary furloughs for tens of thousands of other employees. As part of a broader restructuring, the company's chief executive, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , announced last month that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would cut 17,000 jobs, or 10 percent of its work force.\par \par \uc2 The company recently raised more than $21 billion by selling shares to investors. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  Ratings and \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  both described the fund-raising as a positive step, but said they were still reviewing whether to downgrade \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's credit rating.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  makes most of its commercial airplanes in the Seattle area, including the 737 Max, the 767 and the 777. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  makes its 787 Dreamliner at a nonunion factory in North Charleston, S.C. In all, the company employs more than 66,000 people in Washington State, but it indirectly supports more than twice as many jobs there, according to a report from the Seattle Metropolitan Chamber of Commerce.\par \par \uc2 The Max is \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's most popular commercial jet, accounting for three-fourths of the 6,200 planes the company has on order. But production is far below \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's target because of a crisis that began in January when a panel blew off a Max during an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight.\par \par \uc2 That episode renewed concerns about \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commitment to quality and safety five years after two fatal crashes involving the Max. The panel blowout has led the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  to cap Max production to 38 planes per month until it is satisfied that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has improved its quality practices. Production was still below that number before the strike began. And it will take some time to increase production even after the stoppage ends, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 ''We believe the hangover from the strike will weigh on monthly production of 737 Max aircraft next year,'' the agency said. It will probably take weeks for workers to go through refresher training, \uc2 Moody's\uc2  said, and the uneven supply of parts will most likely remain a problem.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241102ekb20004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
