\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Appeals Court Says Musk Post On Union Push Is Protected\b0\par\par\uc2 By Brooks Barnes \par \uc2 562 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit court ruled that the 2018 post was protected speech. It also vacated an order to reinstate a pro-union \uc2 Tesla\uc2  worker who was fired.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court handed \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  a victory in a freedom-of-speech case on Friday by overturning an earlier ruling in a dispute between the billionaire and the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In March last year, three judges on the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit in New Orleans affirmed the board's finding that \uc2 Tesla\uc2  illegally fired an employee involved in union organizing, and that Mr. Musk, \uc2 Tesla\uc2 's chief executive, had illegally threatened workers' stock options in a post on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  if they chose to unionize. The opinion allowed the labor board to enforce its 2021 order requiring \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to reinstate, with back pay, the employee, Richard Ortiz, and Mr. Musk to delete the 2018 post.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk challenged the panel's ruling, and on Friday the full court ruled, 9 to 8, that the labor board had improperly ordered him to delete the social media post. ''The agency exceeded its authority,'' the 11-page ruling said. ''We hold that Musk's tweets are constitutionally protected speech.''\par \par \uc2 ''Deleting the speech of private citizens on topics of public concern is not a remedy traditionally countenanced by American law,'' the ruling added.\par \par \uc2 The court sent the matter of Mr. Ortiz's firing back to the labor board to review, saying the board had failed ''to consider the fact that the actual decision maker in Ortiz's firing harbored no anti-union animus.''\par \par \uc2 The judges did not rule on whether Mr. Musk's online comment constituted a National Labor Relations Act violation for illegally threatening workers. (The board has held that it did.)\par \par \uc2 In a 31-page dissent joined by seven colleagues, Judge James L. Dennis called the majority opinion ''light on law and facts'' and ''logically incoherent.'' He said he would have ruled that the labor board ''did not exceed or abuse its broad remedial authority'' in ordering Musk to delete the tweet.\par \par \uc2 The labor board declined to comment. \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , Mr. Musk and Mr. Ortiz could not be immediately reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Friday's ruling arose from an incident in 2017 when Mr. Ortiz was involved in an effort to unionize the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  plant in Fremont, Calif. As part of the campaign, he posted screen shots of co-workers who opposed unionization on a private \uc2 Facebook\uc2  page. When an investigator for \uc2 Tesla\uc2  questioned Mr. Ortiz about the photos, he said he couldn't remember where he had gotten them, which he later acknowledged was a lie. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  then fired him, with lying cited as the cause.\par \par \uc2 A labor board investigation found that Mr. Ortiz had been fired because of his unionization campaign, not because of his false statement.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk's post on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , in May 2018, was part of a longer thread about the \uc2 United Automobile Workers union\uc2 . He said there was nothing stopping \uc2 Tesla\uc2  employees at the California plant from voting for a union, adding, ''But why pay union dues & give up stock options for nothing?'' When another commenter asked two days later if Mr. Musk was ''threatening to take away benefits from unionized workers,'' he replied, ''No, \uc2 UAW\uc2  does that.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241028ekas00036\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
