\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Safety Bill for City Hotels Includes Panic Button for Workers\b0\par\par\uc2 By Emma G. Fitzsimmons \par \uc2 654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The bill will require hotels to have licenses, panic buttons for employees and 24-hour staffing at the front desk.\par \par \uc2 After months of contentious negotiations with the hotel industry, the New York City Council overwhelmingly approved a bill on Wednesday aimed at improving safety at hotels for workers and guests.\par \par \uc2 The bill, known as the Safe Hotels Act, will require hotels to obtain licenses in order to operate, and to adhere to the licensing requirements. Hotels must schedule staff at the front desk at all hours, provide panic buttons to employees and keep rooms clean.\par \par \uc2 The bill, which the Council passed 45 to 4, was also supported by powerful industry groups, including the New York Hotel & Gaming Trades Council, which championed the effort, and the Hotel Association of New York City, which had initially opposed it.\par \par \uc2 The bill's sponsor, \uc2 Julie Menin\uc2 , a council member from Manhattan, worked with hotel industry leaders on a compromise after some owners called the bill a ''nuclear bomb'' for the industry and pledged to spend millions of dollars to stop it. Hotels with 100 rooms or fewer will be exempt from a requirement to hire core employees directly; they may still use subcontractors to staff their hotels.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Menin said that it was important for hotels to be regulated like other businesses in the city.\par \par \uc2 ''The bill will protect workers by mandating panic buttons and human-trafficking training, and at the same time protect guests and communities throughout the city through its public safety protections,'' she said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 New York City has more than 700 hotels, and the vast majority are safe. But there have been notable examples of violence, especially at budget hotels outside Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 The bill's passage was a major victory for the Hotel & Gaming Trades Council, which represents about 35,000 hotel and casino workers in New York and New Jersey, and had lobbied hard for it. The union's president, Rich Maroko, said in a statement that it would provide ''sensible health, safety and quality standards for guests and workers.''\par \par \uc2 Mayor Eric Adams, a Democrat who ran on a public safety message, is close with the hotel union and is expected to support the bill. His office did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Adams, who was indicted on federal corruption charges last month, sounded a positive note when asked about the bill in August.\par \par \uc2 ''I love working with the City Council when they talk about how we make our city safer,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The bill has the strong support of the city's five district attorneys, who sent a letter to the Council noting that criminal complaints to the Police Department ''originate from hotels and motels at a far higher rate than from other types of locations.''\par \par \uc2 Operators that violate the license conditions would be subject to civil penalties, including fines of up to $5,000 for multiple offenses. The license fee will be $350.\par \par \uc2 Some hotel groups still oppose the bill and say it would be too onerous on the industry, which is still recovering from the coronavirus pandemic. Kevin Carey, the interim president of the \uc2 American Hotel & Lodging Association\uc2 , a national industry group, said the bill would harm minority-owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 Its passage ''caps a legislative scramble and special interest power play that will do irreparable harm to the city's hotel industry and tourism economy,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Vijay Dandapani\uc2 , president of the Hotel Association of New York City, which represents nearly 300 hotels with more than 80,000 rooms collectively, had vehemently opposed the bill but now supports it.\par \par \uc2 ''Because of changes we negotiated to this legislation that allow for fairer licensing rules and exempt small hotels from unworkable mandates, we now believe it will create practical standards that will protect our industry and its workers,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241024ekao0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
