\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Boeing Union Plans Vote That Could End Its Strike\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 798 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The deal reached by the machinists union, which represents more than 33,000 workers, would cumulatively raise wages by nearly 40 percent over four years, according to details shared by the union.\par \par \uc2 Leaders of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest union said on Saturday that they had reached a ''negotiated proposal'' for a new contract and would put it up for a vote to end a long and expensive strike.\par \par \uc2 In a post on its website on Saturday, the union said that ''with the help of Acting U.S. Secretary of Labor Julie Su,'' it had reached a deal that ''warrants presenting to the members and is worthy of your consideration.''\par \par \uc2 The strike by the union's more than 33,000 members, who mostly build commercial airplanes in the Seattle area, began on Sept. 13 and has taken a notable toll on \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , which was already in difficult financial straits. Within days of the walkout, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  announced cost-cutting measures, including temporary furloughs for tens of thousands of white-collar employees.\par \par \uc2 The proposal, which was agreed to after multiple rounds of failed talks, would raise wages cumulatively by nearly 40 percent over the four-year life of the contract, according to details shared by the union. That is a significant increase over the previous proposal and close to the amount the union had initially sought. The deal also includes a bonus of $7,000 should workers ratify the deal, and the reinstatement of performance bonuses that were set to be cut.\par \par \uc2 The union, the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2 , said that the vote on the proposal would take place on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said, ''We look forward to our employees voting on the negotiated proposal.''\par \par \uc2 If members vote for the contract, it would replace one that was agreed to in 2008 after a two-month strike. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  later said in securities filings that the strike contributed to decline in revenue that year of about $6.4 billion because it delivered 104 fewer planes than expected.\par \par \uc2 In its first week, the current strike cost the company at least $571 million, according to an analysis by Anderson Economic Group, a Michigan-based research and consulting firm.\par \par \uc2 The company said in regulatory filings on Tuesday that it could raise as much as $25 billion by selling debt or stock over the next three years and that it had entered into a $10 billion credit agreement with a group of banks, an effort to improve its financial position as costs mounted. Earlier this month, the company announced a restructuring that included plans to cut 17,000 jobs, or 10 percent of its work force.\par \par \uc2 Washington State\uc2  is home to most of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial airplane production. It makes the 737 Max at a factory in Renton and the 767 and 777 in Everett. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  also produces the 787 Dreamliner at a factory in North Charleston, S.C., but workers there are not represented by a union.\par \par \uc2 The Max is by far \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's most popular commercial plane, accounting for more than three-fourths of the approximately 5,500 planes in the company's order book. But output is far lower than \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would like.\par \par \uc2 The company was forced to slow production of the jet to make quality improvements after a panel fell off a Max during an \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2  flight in January, renewing concerns about \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commitment to quality and safety five years after two fatal crashes involving the 737 Max.\par \par \uc2 Airline executives say they are pleased with the progress \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has made since that January flight, but the strike represented a setback for the company and its new chief executive, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , a former leader of aerospace supplier \uc2 Rockwell Collins\uc2 , who joined \uc2 Boeing\uc2  in August.\par \par \uc2 When they put the previous contract offer to a membership vote, union leaders described the proposal in glowing terms. That proposal included a 25 percent raise, an increase in 401(k) contributions from \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and a commitment to build a new commercial plane in the Seattle area. But more than 95 percent of members who voted on the deal rejected it.\par \par \uc2 When negotiations began in March, the union said it was seeking a 40 percent raise and the reinstatement of pension benefits lost nearly a decade ago, an important issue for many workers. Union members are also resentful of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's decision to move the assembly of the 787 from Washington to South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  employs more than 66,000 people in Washington, but supports more than twice as many jobs in the state when considering its effect on other businesses, according to the Seattle Metropolitan Chamber of Commerce. The company paused spending on parts after the strike began.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241020ekak0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
