\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 The Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ravi Mattu and The Associated Press \par \uc2 510 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 RETAIL\par \par \uc2 LVMH\uc2  Shares Plummet\par \par \uc2 At Disappointing Earnings\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 LVMH\uc2  dropped on Wednesday after the luxury goods giant warned about an ''uncertain economic and geopolitical environment'' and its latest earnings disappointed analysts.\par \par \uc2 The conglomerate -- which owns Dior, Tiffany, Fendi and more -- is a bellwether for the industry. Its financial results, released on Tuesday after European markets closed, sent a shudder through the luxury sector, particularly in response to slowing sales in the hugely important Chinese market.\par \par \uc2 LVMH\uc2 , which is run by the French billionaire \uc2 Bernard Arnault\uc2 , far right, said sales for last quarter fell 3 percent from a year earlier. The company also reported a decline in sales in its fashion and leather goods unit, which makes up about half of the conglomerate's revenue, for the first time since early in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Shares of other fashion and lifestyle brands, including Herm\u195\'00\'C3\u168\'00\'A8s and Kering, the owner of Gucci, also declined.\par \par \uc2 Investors are jittery about the Chinese economy. Last month, Beijing introduced measures that spurred a major rally in Chinese stocks, but details remain vague about the extent of the measures to bolster weak consumer spending, stabilize the real estate market and strengthen banks.\par \par \uc2 China recently announced retaliatory penalties on European brandy -- \uc2 LVMH\uc2  owns Mo\u195\'00\'C3\u171\'00\'ABt Hennessy -- in response to higher tariffs imposed by the \uc2 European Union\uc2  on Chinese-made electric vehicles. RAVI MATTU\par \par \uc2 AVIATION\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  Lays Off 2,500\par \par \uc2 In Reset of Space Division\par \par \uc2 The European aircraft maker and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  rival \uc2 Airbus\uc2  said on Wednesday that it was laying off 2,500 workers as it tries to turn around its struggling defense and space division.\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  said it would implement other organizational changes as it faces ongoing challenges in the defense and space sector, including disrupted supply chains, the rapid evolution of warfare tactics and increasing costs.\par \par \uc2 The announcement comes after the company had already begun making organizational changes in the division last year, which it said have begun ''bearing fruit.''\par \par \uc2 ''We want to shape the division so it can act as a leading and competitive player in this ever-evolving market,'' said \uc2 Mike Schoellhorn\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Airbus\uc2 's defense and space division. ''This requires us to become faster, leaner and more competitive.''\par \par \uc2 The company's defense and space business struggled last year, notably taking a 477 million euro, or $511 million, loss on the long-troubled A400M military transport plane, in part because of high inflation.\par \par \uc2 The European space sector also was hit by the loss of access to Russia's Soyuz rocket launchers and the failure of a new Vega-C rocket soon after takeoff from French Guiana in late 2022.\par \par \uc2 Outside of that division, business has been booming for the aerospace giant. \uc2 Airbus\uc2  has outpaced \uc2 Boeing\uc2  for five straight years in plane orders and deliveries and its profits have soared.\par \par \uc2 The bigger problem for \uc2 Airbus\uc2  has been keeping up with demand for commercial airplanes. As of June, the manufacturer had an order backlog of 8,585 commercial aircraft. Associated Press\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241017ekah0004w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
