\par\par \uc2 DEALBOOK NEWSLETTER\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Hybrid Work Fails to Sway Executives, Despite Data\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Hirsch \par \uc2 1,839 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies like \uc2 Amazon\uc2  have required a return to the office five days a week despite findings showing benefits to employers that allow some remote days.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's C.E.O., \uc2 Andy Jassy\uc2 , made waves last month when he demanded that all employees return to the office five days a week. The proclamation seemed to validate similar demands made by executives like \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2 's \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2  and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 's \uc2 David Solomon\uc2 . And it naturally raised the question of whether others might follow suit. (It appears some have.)\par \par \uc2 But it also flew in the face of researchers and their studies that have found hybrid work benefits companies. Stanford's Nick Bloom, for example, has found that employees who work two days a week at home are just as productive and less likely to quit. (Bloom, like others, speculated that \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's pronouncement was really an attempt to reduce the work force without official layoffs. An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  spokeswoman said the policy is ''an effort to strengthen our culture and ensure our teams are connected. She added that the company has no plans for the decision to reduce overall head count.)\par \par \uc2 So why do so many employers that say they're data-driven seem to move counter to science?\par \par \uc2 Executives are not convinced by the research. ''It's not like: 'Aspirin definitely helps with headaches. It's been proven again and again and again,''' \uc2 Laszlo Bock\uc2 , a former senior vice president for people operations at \uc2 Google\uc2 , told DealBook. ''The academic studies that have been done, and there are not that many, show a range of outcomes -- and they generally show a kind of neutral to slightly positive.''\par \par \uc2 Adam Grant, an organizational psychologist at Wharton, said he disagreed, pointing DealBook to a meta-analysis of 108 studies.\par \par \uc2 Some are just over it. Almost five years since the start of the pandemic, many C.E.O.s are ready to move on from an experiment they never wanted to start. ''When we look back over the last five years, we continue to believe that the advantages of being together in the office are significant,'' Jassy wrote in a memo about ending remote work at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Grant says C.E.O.s may not always methodically control for whether an effect was caused by remote work, the pandemic or something else, as an academic researcher would.\par \par \uc2 They also may not want to find out that hybrid work is better.\par \par \uc2 ''Leadership is a lot less fulfilling and a lot less fun when you're doing it remotely,'' Bock said.\par \par \uc2 They see their company as different. Many of the executives who have called employees back to the office point to the particularities of their business or industry that require in-person work.\par \par \uc2 That includes Jassy. ''Our culture is unique,'' he wrote in the memo.\par \par \uc2 This predisposition to dismiss outside data is common. ''When we replicated studies in different industries, in different organizations, the patterns look pretty similar,'' Grant said. ''And when I talk to C.E.O.s, they all have the same struggles and challenges regardless of an industry.''\par \par \uc2 It's not practical. Hybrid work may be technically the best route, but it's also complicated to oversee. Time is money, and C.E.O.s may not want to spend theirs deliberating over a decision about bringing workers back -- or figuring out how to manage a hybrid workplace.\par \par \uc2 But that, Grant argues, may be missing the point.\par \par \uc2 ''There's a premium on decisiveness in the business world,'' he said. But ''if you're making a highly consequential, irreversible decision, you are supposed to slow down as much as possible.''\par \par \uc2 -- Lauren Hirsch\par \par \uc2 IN CASE YOU MISSED IT\par \par \uc2 Big banks reported better-than-expected results to kick off earnings season. \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  and \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  beat analysts' forecasts even as worries persist about the health of the economy. The results boosted the companies' share prices, but \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 's C.E.O., warned that growing geopolitical risks in the Middle East and Ukraine could hurt the global economy.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department weighs trying to break up \uc2 Google\uc2 . Regulators laid out potential remedies after the company was declared ''a monopolist'' in online search, including forcing the technology group to spin off one of its businesses. But any move to force \uc2 Google\uc2  to split could be difficult to achieve, with legal experts noting that the Justice Department would need to prove that other changes would not solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Milton pummeled Florida, causing billions of dollars in damage. The storm left more than 2.5 million people without power after rolling through the state.\par \par \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's \uc2 Rick Rieder\uc2  wants more rate cuts, for everyone's sake\par \par \uc2 Fall, and the fourth quarter for corporate America, has only just begun. But the financial world has already gone through several seasons: from fears of an economic slowdown to relieved optimism about the labor market -- and now some worries over whether inflation is fully vanquished.\par \par \uc2 Rick Rieder\uc2  leads the global asset allocation team at \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 , which reported on Friday that it had brought in $221 billion during the third quarter, its most ever, and that its total assets under management had reached $11.5 trillion. He feels that the overall picture -- and correct course of action -- is already clear. In his eyes, inflation is largely resolved and the Fed needs to keep cutting rates, even if recession risks remain low.\par \par \uc2 In a conversation with The Times's Talmon Joseph Smith at \uc2 BlackRock\uc2 's Hudson Yards headquarters, Mr. Rieder doubled down on his outlook. This interview has been edited and condensed for clarity.\par \par \uc2 You have said that it might be better to think of the U.S. economy as a satellite, sort of always in orbit, rather than as a plane approaching a hard or soft landing. Some business-cycle traditionalists shake their heads at that.\par \par \uc2 Satellites don't land. They just get tired over time, and they need a bit more energy. I really believe in this. The Fed raises rates 500 basis points and it doesn't make a difference, and it's because the service economy is not that cyclical. Goods are hugely cyclical.\par \par \uc2 But the reason why I think the Fed's still going to cut rates more is this. Look at what's happening to low-income people. Look at credit card delinquencies, auto loan delinquencies, mortgage rates. You've got to get that rate down.\par \par \uc2 Some people who don't think the Fed needs to cut at all echo your service economy point, arguing it can actually keep coasting along without further cuts.\par \par \uc2 I think you have a two-tiered economy. Companies are doing great. They termed all their debt out already. Baby boomers, higher income, doing great.\par \par \uc2 For the rest of the country, you have no cash, but the money is in your house. So if we keep the mortgage rate around 7 percent, what happens is homes are unaffordable and there's not enough inventory. If the Fed brings the rate down, you stop brutalizing the low income and small businesses. Shelter inflation is the only thing that's high. And it's high because there's not enough homes on the market.\par \par \uc2 Well, then, how fast should they cut and how much, in your view?\par \par \uc2 I think they should go another 25 basis points in November. I think they'll go 25 in December. And then I don't think we need to go that far after.\par \par \uc2 What do you think about housing supply now being a major presidential campaign issue?\par \par \uc2 I think some of the policy proposals that would create incentives for home building -- and first-time home buyer incentives -- are really attractive. It brings down rent prices and addresses the inventory problem.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of the ongoing A.I. race?\par \par \uc2 We are going to go through this incredible mountain of spending on A.I. and infrastructure. You get to the other side, and then, boom, the next five, 10, 20 years, you reap the benefits of it -- with labor costs being the primary form.\par \par \uc2 I've seen studies showing 30 percent to 60 percent of jobs can be augmented or replaced. And I think people underestimate it.\par \par \uc2 Do you fear that after that peak spending on A.I., we will have a dot-com-bubble-like crash when that wheel stops spinning?\par \par \uc2 If you said to me today would I still invest in tech, yes. I don't think the multiples on tech stocks are that high. Those companies will become a bigger and bigger part of the corporate universe. I just think that keeps going.\par \par \uc2 It's hard from an asset-allocation point of view. How much do you want to have in seven stocks?\par \par \uc2 The activist at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 's gates\par \par \uc2 Few activist investors nowadays are as ambitious, or as busy, as \uc2 Starboard Value\uc2 . The hedge fund is pushing for change at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  while also trying to shake up \uc2 News Corp\uc2 , Match and others.\par \par \uc2 While Starboard has had quiet successes, the firm has also waged many memorably public, heated battles. It's not clear yet which way the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  campaign will go, but if it becomes a drag-out fight, investors can expect some zingers. Here's a sampling from previous battles.\par \par \uc2 Darden: In one of Starboard's most famous fights, it sought to completely replace the restaurant operator's board. The hedge fund argued in 2014 that Darden, which owns Olive Garden and other chains, was being mismanaged and -- in a nearly 300-page presentation that has become the stuff of Wall Street legend -- accused it of churning out substandard pasta:\par \par \uc2 Shockingly, Olive Garden no longer salts the water it uses to boil the pasta, merely to get a longer warranty on its pots. This appalling decision shows just how little regard management has for delivering a quality experience to guests.\par \par \uc2 Starboard succeeded in kicking out all of Darden's directors, a rare accomplishment by any activist. (But Darden did not start salting its pasta water.)\par \par \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 : Starboard got involved in the embattled web pioneer amid its long business struggles under \uc2 Marissa Mayer\uc2 , its C.E.O. Mayer reportedly struck a secret agreement with Starboard in 2015 to limit costs at \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 , in exchange for the hedge fund's withdrawal of its demand for board seats.\par \par \uc2 But spending at \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  instead grew, and the company said it planned to spin off its huge and incredibly valuable stake in the Chinese internet giant \uc2 Alibaba\uc2 . An indignant Starboard then sought to overthrow most of the board:\par \par \uc2 For over a year, we have attempted to work constructively with management and the board of \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 . We have tried extremely hard to work ''behind the scenes.'' We have grown increasingly frustrated.\par \par \uc2 Yahoo\uc2  eventually settled with Starboard, giving the hedge fund four board seats.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for reading! We'll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We'd like your feedback. Please email thoughts and suggestions to dealbook@nytimes.com.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241014ekae00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
