\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Workers And Boeing Digging In For Fight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Niraj Chokshi \par \uc2 1,480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly a month into a union walkout, the aerospace giant withdrew its latest contract offer, and the two sides exchanged blame over the breakdown.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and its largest union appear to be digging in for a long fight -- even as some striking workers start to look for temporary jobs and the company risks having its credit rating downgraded to junk status.\par \par \uc2 Nearly a month into the strike, negotiations between \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and the union resumed this week under federal mediation after a long break. But they collapsed on Tuesday with the company withdrawing its latest offer. The two sides traded blame for the breakdown.\par \par \uc2 In a message to employees, \uc2 Stephanie Pope\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's commercial airplane unit, said the union had made ''demands far in excess of what can be accepted if we are to remain competitive as a business.''\par \par \uc2 The union accused \uc2 Boeing\uc2  of being ''hellbent'' on sticking to the offer that labor leaders had previously rejected for being insufficient to garner the support of most of its more than 33,000 members.\par \par \uc2 A long strike is the last thing \uc2 Boeing\uc2  needs. The company, which hasn't reported a full-year profit since 2018, is now losing tens of millions of dollars more every day that striking workers are not building planes. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is also trying to persuade regulators to let it produce more 737 Max jets, its best-selling plane. And on Tuesday, \uc2 S&P Global Ratings\uc2  said it was considering lowering the company's credit rating, which sits just above junk status, depending on the strike's length.\par \par \uc2 The walkout, which began on Sept. 13, is also difficult for workers, many of whom are living off savings and have had to find health coverage after \uc2 Boeing\uc2  dropped them from its plan this month.\par \par \uc2 But in interviews and online forums, many workers said they were prepared to wait for a better deal after growing frustrated by wages that failed to keep pace with rising costs and concessions that they made in previous contracts. They have also drawn inspiration from the gains that unions in other industries have secured for their members.\par \par \uc2 ''The union membership is really unified right now,'' said Rob Tompkins, a quality assurance inspector on the 777X plane in Everett, Wash. ''I'm ready to stay out until January if need be.''\par \par \uc2 The strike began after workers overwhelmingly rejected a four-year tentative contract negotiated by union leaders and company executives. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  made the now-withdrawn proposal days later, offering a 30 percent raise over four years and other improved benefits, but the union and its members said the deal didn't go far enough.\par \par \uc2 A survey of thousands of members at the time found that a vast majority were not interested in the offer, Jon Holden, the leader of District 751 of the Machinists union, which represents nearly all of the striking workers, said in an interview.\par \par \uc2 ''The reality is it didn't meet what our members were looking for,'' he said. ''We were never going to vote that offer.''\par \par \uc2 Labor negotiations are a test of wills, and \uc2 Boeing\uc2  may be banking on the fact that a longer strike will weaken the resolve of the union and its members, said Harry Katz, a professor of collective bargaining at Cornell University's School of Industrial and Labor Relations.\par \par \uc2 ''I suspect they're hoping that as the strike continues on somewhat longer, the workers and the union will lower their expectations,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Katz also said it was unlikely that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would agree to one of the main demands of many union members -- a restoration of a pension that was frozen about a decade ago. Under investor pressure, companies have spent decades ditching pensions that promise consistent payouts because they can be expensive for employers. Instead, they've shifted to retirement plans like 401(k)s that don't offer guaranteed payouts.\par \par \uc2 The machinists union has tried to prepare for a lengthy strike. It recently started paying workers $250 a week from a strike fund and, for years, has encouraged members to save, Mr. Holden said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Tompkins, who joined the company in 2011, said he had been saving, cutting spending and working overtime. His colleagues have taken temporary jobs like driving for \uc2 Uber\uc2 , moving goods at \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses and stocking grocery stores.\par \par \uc2 ''We all knew this was coming,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Jason Richards works on heating, cooling and ventilation systems at a \uc2 Boeing\uc2  parts facility in Auburn, Wash. His wife works as a manager at a catering company, which helps, he said, though the couple have five children, ages 9 to 16.\par \par \uc2 ''I have a little nest egg saved up,'' he said. ''But ideally, I wouldn't be making a major dent in that.''\par \par \uc2 They and other workers said they needed higher wages because costs had gone up a lot.\par \par \uc2 Consumer prices in the Seattle area have risen more than 40 percent over the past decade, according to federal data. The region's thriving tech industry, which includes \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , has contributed to fast-rising home prices, which have more than doubled over that same period, according to the Case-Shiller index.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  machinists make about $75,000 in average annual pay, which includes 8 percent in contractual raises over the last decade and more than $4 an hour in additional cost-of-living adjustments over the same period, according to the company. The union is seeking a 40 percent wage increase in a new contract.\par \par \uc2 ''Real estate is the key cost that has just exploded here,'' said Thomas Gilbert, a professor of finance and economics at the University of Washington's Foster School of Business in Seattle. ''If wages only increase at 2 percent a year or 3 percent a year compared to real estate, it simply has not kept up at all.''\par \par \uc2 Under \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's most recent offer, starting pay for union members, most of whom work in the Seattle suburbs, would have risen to $20 an hour, and the average machinist would have earned more than $111,000 annually by the end of the four-year contract, according to the company. That would have put starting pay at around Seattle's minimum wage, which is slated to rise to $20.76 in January.\par \par \uc2 Nationally, machinists on average earn almost $55,000 a year, according to the latest federal data. In the Seattle area, the average machinist made more than $70,000.\par \par \uc2 Striking \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers say other blue-collar workers, including those at car factories and the ports, have secured big gains in recently negotiated labor contracts.\par \par \uc2 Those successes, along with \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's recent struggles, have given the machinists confidence that they can wait the company out to win a great contract. ''We are at maximum leverage, maximum power, maximum solidarity,'' Mr. Holden said.\par \par \uc2 Many \uc2 Boeing\uc2  workers are also seeking changes to a 2014 deal in which the union's members accepted a contract that froze their pension plan in exchange for a promise by \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to keep production of the 777X in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was publicly searching for a different place to make the plane, which is designed for international flights. \uc2 Washington State\uc2  ultimately approved $8.7 billion in subsidies to encourage the company to keep the work in the Seattle area.\par \par \uc2 Several workers said they felt that \uc2 Boeing\uc2  had bullied the union into that deal, even as the company earned record profits. They also expressed frustration with their union's international leadership for forcing a vote early in January when some more senior workers would have been on vacation. Union members approved the deal, 51 percent to 49 percent.\par \par \uc2 ''I think the company vastly underestimated how upset the membership was from the 2014 contract extension,'' said Andrew DeFreese, a heavy-equipment operator at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Everett plant. ''We have been talking about that ever since it happened.''\par \par \uc2 This time, some workers said they were in a strong position because the company had lurched from crisis to crisis in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Five years ago, two 737 Max planes crashed, killing 346 people. Regulators grounded the plane for nearly two years until \uc2 Boeing\uc2  fixed the software implicated in the crashes. The crisis cost billions of dollars and damaged the company's reputation.\par \par \uc2 Then, during the coronavirus pandemic, supply chain chaos limited \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's ability to make planes. This January, a panel blew off a Max jet during a flight, renewing concerns about quality and safety.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has made many changes, including increasing training and oversight. A new chief executive, \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2 , took over in August.\par \par \uc2 But the company still faces significant challenges. Its debt totals nearly $58 billion, up from about $9 billion a decade ago. And the \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2  has limited how many Max jets \uc2 Boeing\uc2  can produce until it improves production quality.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241010ekaa0003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
