\par\par \uc2 Money and Business/Financial Desk; SECTBU\par \b \uc2 47,000\b0\par\par\uc2 By Santul Nerkar \par \uc2 478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The dockworkers on the East and Gulf Coasts who were briefly on strike.\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , the union that represents 47,000 dockworkers who load and unload container ships on the East and Gulf Coasts, went on strike on Tuesday, a threat to the economy just weeks before the presidential election. But two days later, on Thursday evening, the strike had ended.\par \par \uc2 The union reached an agreement with the group representing port employers after the group, the United States Maritime Alliance, offered the dockworkers a 62 percent pay increase over the course of a new six-year contract.\par \par \uc2 Wages had been a major issue, along with the union's demand for restrictions on the use of automation at the ports, which members feared could eliminate their jobs. Both sides agreed to extend the current contract until Jan. 15, and the union said it would return to the bargaining table ''to negotiate all other outstanding issues.''\par \par \uc2 Had the strike dragged on, it could have snarled supply chains and led to shortages and higher prices for certain goods. It also threatened to result in layoffs, not just for the dockworkers, but for workers employed in the industries that depend on the flow of exports and imports.\par \par \uc2 ''If they stop working, the goods stop moving,'' said William Brucher, an assistant professor at the Rutgers School of Management and Labor Relations. ''They have real economic power and leverage.''\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers tend to be higher paid than other blue-collar workers. With the agreement on Thursday, the highest-paid longshoreman will make $63 an hour at the end of a new six-year contract, up from the current $39 an hour. At the Port of New York and New Jersey, nearly 60 percent of dockworkers made $100,000 to $200,000 in the 12 months through June 2020.\par \par \uc2 ''I cannot recall an episode that had so little effect on the economy, led to such a short strike and resulted in such a huge increase in earnings for workers who are already making over $100,000 a year,'' said \uc2 Patrick L. Anderson\uc2 , the chief executive of the consultancy Anderson Economic Group.\par \par \uc2 ''Broadly, we have to protect the level playing field in all the different sectors of our industry.''\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  of France, on why he was supporting higher tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 ''Inflation has moved down, and unemployment has moved up, in both cases significantly.''\par \par \uc2 Jerome H. Powell\uc2 , the chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , suggesting that the central bank would cut rates two more times in 2024.\par \par \uc2 ''Hopefully, there won't be a time when we look back sorry for this effort.''\par \par \uc2 Representative Zoe Lofgren of California, on President Biden's plans to sign a bill that would allow semiconductor manufacturing projects to skip a key environmental review.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241006eka60005d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
