\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 An Unexpectedly Strong Jobs Report Sends the Markets Higher\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 700 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rallied Friday after a surprisingly strong report on the U.S. job market raised optimism about the economy.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 climbed 0.9 percent and got close to its high set on Monday. The Dow Jones industrial average rose 341 points, or 0.8 percent, to set its own record, while the Nasdaq composite moved 1.2 percent higher.\par \par \uc2 Leading the way were banks, airlines, cruise-ship operators and other companies whose profits can benefit the most from a stronger economy where people are working and better able to pay for things. \uc2 Norwegian Cruise Line\uc2  steamed 4.9 percent higher, \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  rose 3.5 percent and the small companies in the Russell 2000 index gained 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 They helped stock indexes claw back losses from earlier in the week, caused by worries that worsening tensions in the Middle East could lead to disruptions in the global flow of oil. Crude prices rose again Friday, but the moves were more modest than earlier in the week, as the world continued its wait to see how Israel will respond to Iran's missile attack.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the strength of the U.S. economy reclaimed its spot as the top mover of markets.\par \par \uc2 Treasury yields soared in the bond market after the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  said employers added 254,000 more jobs to their payrolls last month than they cut. That was an acceleration from August's hiring pace of 159,000 and blew past economists' forecasts.\par \par \uc2 It was a ''grand slam'' of a report, according to Lindsay Rosner, head of multi-sector investing within \uc2 Goldman Sachs Asset Management\uc2 . She said policymakers at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , who have been trying to pull off the difficult feat of keeping the economy humming while getting inflation under control, ''must be smiling.''\par \par \uc2 Friday's report capped a week of mostly encouraging data on the economy, helping to allay one of Wall Street's top questions: Can the job market continue to hold up after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  earlier kept interest rates at a two-decade high?\par \par \uc2 Before Friday's jobs report, the general trend had been a slowdown in hiring by U.S. employers. That's not surprising given how hard the Fed pressed the brakes on the economy through higher rates in order to stamp out high inflation.\par \par \uc2 But Friday's blowout numbers bolstered hope that the U.S. economy will keep growing, particularly now that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has begun cutting interest rates to give it more juice. The Fed last month lowered its main interest rate for the first time in more than four years and indicated more cuts will arrive through next year.\par \par \uc2 Friday's jobs report was so strong that it pushed traders to abandon bets that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will deliver another larger-than-usual cut to interest rates at its next meeting. They're now forecasting zero chance for a cut of half a percentage point, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 . Just a week ago, they were saying it was better than a coin flip's chance.\par \par \uc2 Such diminished expectations for future cuts sent the yield on the two-year Treasury shooting up to 3.93 percent from 3.71 percent late Thursday. The 10-year yield jumped to 3.98 percent from 3.85 percent.\par \par \uc2 The forced rethink about how low rates will ultimately go hurt stocks of home builders, real-estate owners and other companies that benefit from easier mortgage rates.\par \par \uc2 D.R. Horton\uc2 , \uc2 PulteGroup\uc2  and \uc2 Lennar\uc2  all sank at least 2.5 percent for three of the biggest losses in the S&P 500. \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  slipped 0.8 percent and was the biggest single reason the Dow Jones industrial average lagged other indexes. During the day, the Dow went from an early gain of 300 points to a modest loss and back to a big gain.\par \par \uc2 All told, the S&P 500 rose 51.13 points to 5,751.07. The Dow gained 341.16 to 42,352.75, and the Nasdaq climbed 219.37 to 18,137.85.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241005eka50004j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
