\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Days after longshoremen halted work, their union and their bosses have a tentative deal on wages.\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Eavis \par \uc2 1,322 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Days after tens of thousands of longshoreman along the East and Gulf Coasts walked out, their union and their bosses reached a tentative agreement on wages.\par \par \uc2 Hours after a longshoremen's union on the East and Gulf Coasts agreed to suspend its strike, major ports rushed to reopen on Friday and get cargo to businesses that have spent the last few days racked with fear over lost sales.\par \par \uc2 The strike, which began on Tuesday and shut down many of the nation's largest shipping hubs, threatened to weaken the economy weeks ahead of a national election. The Biden administration spent the last few days pressing the United States Maritime Alliance, the group representing port employers, and the union, the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , to find a way to end the strike.\par \par \uc2 The two sides announced late Thursday that they had reached a tentative agreement on wages -- a 62 percent increase over six years -- and said they were extending the current contract until mid-January to negotiate other issues. The biggest remaining one is the use of automated machinery at the ports, which the I.L.A. considers a job killer. It was the first full-scale stoppage at East and Gulf Coast ports since 1977.\par \par \uc2 Labor experts say the I.L.A. has more leverage in contract negotiations than unions in most other industries, because a walkout can shut down shipping facilities for which there are no practical alternatives. Labor experts said the wage increase was a big victory for the I.L.A. and its combative president, Harold J. Daggett, a 78-year-old, third-generation dockworker.\par \par \uc2 ''The I.L.A. seized the moment,'' said William Brucher, an assistant professor at the Rutgers School of Management and Labor Relations. ''Things really aligned in their favor.''\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the strike was unlikely to lead to higher prices for consumers. Many businesses, anticipating the walkout, sped up their shipments so they could receive them before this week, softening the blow for many.\par \par \uc2 But some remained concerned that the backup from the three-day shutdown could cause delays in receiving goods in time for the holiday season.\par \par \uc2 John Wrenn, the chief operating officer at MHW, an alcoholic beverages distributor in Manhasset, N.Y., said dozens of his shipments had been delayed because they were sitting on ships outside the Port of New York and New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 ''People are getting back to work. The impact to us is greatly reduced,'' he said. ''But we are missing sales opportunities on these products.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wrenn said he had shipped in a small amount of goods by air to ensure he could meet customers' orders, but airfreight, he said, is five to 10 times as expensive as using ocean carriers.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, two dozen commercial vessels were waiting to enter the Port of New York and New Jersey -- including 19 container ships, the port's director, \uc2 Beth Rooney\uc2 , said in a news conference.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Rooney said she expected the logjam to clear quickly, noting that there had been several occasions when winter storms closed the port for three or four days and that recovery had been quick. She also disputed some analysts' projections that a day's backlog at a port could take a week to clear.\par \par \uc2 ''Having done this several times in my 35-year career, I have not seen it,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Once the cargo is unloaded at a port, it has to be transported to its destination by truck or train. J. Bruce Chan, a transportation analyst at \uc2 Stifel\uc2 , a Wall Street firm, said there was ample truck capacity right now to handle the extra loads.\par \par \uc2 ''It probably is going to be barely discernible, especially for the end consumer,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Supply chain experts say they are most concerned about the impact on global shipping, which has gone through wrenching disruptions in recent years. Most recently, the Houthi attacks on shipping in the Red Sea prompted ocean carriers to stop using the Suez Canal and take a much longer route around the Cape of Good Hope in South Africa.\par \par \uc2 The diversion has forced shipping companies to deploy more vessels to try to deliver goods on time. And by causing vessels to idle off the East and Gulf Coasts, the strike may have caused shipping capacity to become more strained.\par \par \uc2 ''Because you had the delay of vessels here, you're going to have a delay in getting those ships back to Asia,'' said Greg Miller, a senior maritime reporter for Lloyd's List, a trade publication. He said that 60 cargo ships were waiting outside the East and Gulf Coast ports on Thursday, and that he expected the number to rise in the coming days, even as ports began to resume activity.\par \par \uc2 When congestion was its worst at the ports, in 2022, around 100 vessels were waiting to dock, Mr. Miller said.\par \par \uc2 Congestion can push up ocean freight costs.\par \par \uc2 The cost of shipping a container is 250 percent higher than it was a year ago, according to an index from \uc2 Freightos\uc2 , a digital shipping marketplace, pushed higher by the Houthi attacks, which started at the end of last year.\par \par \uc2 Longshoremen with several years of experience earn a lot more than workers in other professions that don't require a college degree. At the Port of New York and New Jersey, nearly 60 percent of the longshoremen made $100,000 to $200,000 in the 12 months that ended in June 2020, according to the latest figures available from the agency that helped oversee the port.\par \par \uc2 Increasing wages by 62 percent -- as proposed in the tentative agreement -- could push up port costs that may be passed onto the businesses that ship goods, some economists said.\par \par \uc2 ''I cannot recall an episode that had so little effect on the economy, led to such a short strike and resulted in such a huge increase in earnings for workers who are already making over $100,000 a year,'' said \uc2 Patrick L. Anderson\uc2 , the chief executive of the Anderson Economic Group, a consultancy. ''We tend to shrug off the costs, but it does affect our ability to build things and export them.''\par \par \uc2 Longshoremen defend their big paychecks, saying they work long hours for them, often in harsh conditions. They also assert that they are asking for a small portion of the outsize profits that the port employers made during the pandemic trade boom in 2021 and 2022.\par \par \uc2 ''We are humbly asking and boldly insisting that we share in a sliver of the benefits,'' said Leonard Riley, a longshoreman for nearly 50 years at the Port of Charleston in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The big question now is whether the union and the Maritime Alliance can reach a permanent deal by mid-January. Though a wage increase has been agreed to, there is still much that divides the two sides.\par \par \uc2 The current contract gives the I.L.A. some protection against automation, banning equipment that is ''devoid of human interaction.'' But that provision has not stopped operators at some East Coast ports from introducing large cranes that can sort containers without a person directing their moves.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the union may press for tighter language that port employers may resist, believing automation is necessary to make their facilities more efficient.\par \par \uc2 ''I think both sides are pretty far apart on the automation issue,'' Mr. Chan, the analyst, said.\par \par \uc2 An I.L.A. spokesman did not comment on the talks. In a statement, the Maritime Alliance said, ''We have a tentative agreement on wages that is contingent on us bargaining all open issues -- including technology.''\par \par \uc2 Many longshoremen believe that manned equipment can operate as effectively as automated machinery.\par \par \uc2 ''If we are talking about productivity, we are delivering that faster than the places that are much more automated than we are,'' Mr. Riley said.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241005eka500044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
