\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 After Fears Of Recession, New Crises\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Rappeport and Ana Swanson \par \uc2 1,119 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 War in the Middle East, a strike by port workers and a devastating hurricane injected uncertainty into the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 The United States economy is suddenly staring down new and potentially damaging crises, with tensions flaring in the Middle East and several states grappling with fallout from a devastating hurricane.\par \par \uc2 The events hit just as American policymakers were gaining confidence that they had successfully tamed inflation without pushing the economy into a recession and as polls and consumer surveys suggested that Americans' sour economic mood had begun to improve. But in just a week, new risks have emerged.\par \par \uc2 The economy now faces the prospect of an oil price spike and the aftermath of a storm that could inflict more than $100 billion in damage upon large swaths of the Southeast. Economists have also been tracking potential consequences of a port workers' strike, which was suspended on Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 ''There's new uncertainty,'' said \uc2 Joseph E. Gagnon\uc2 , senior fellow at the \uc2 Peterson Institute for International Economics\uc2 . ''If we lose oil output in the Middle East, if the ports are not functioning, then both are inflationary.''\par \par \uc2 That uncertainty is arriving just weeks before a presidential election in which the economy -- in particular, inflation -- is one of the biggest factors on voters' minds and less than a month after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  began cutting interest rates from more than a two-decade high. The central bank has gained confidence that inflation is coming back to its 2 percent goal, but has been wary about the labor market weakening.\par \par \uc2 Even before the new risks emerged, the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  was projecting that the U.S. economy would slow next year.\par \par \uc2 The escalation of the conflict in the Middle East is the most worrying scenario for the world economy. Economists have warned for nearly a year that if the fighting between Israel and \uc2 Hamas\uc2  in Gaza became a regional war, it could cause an oil price shock that could reignite inflation around the globe.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 World Bank\uc2  said last October that its worst-case scenario was an outcome similar to the 1973 Arab oil embargo, which took place during the Arab-Israeli war. A disruption of that severity could remove as much as eight million barrels of oil per day from the market and send prices as high as $157 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 This week, oil prices jumped more than 8 percent after Iran launched nearly 200 missiles at Israel, which vowed to retaliate. They surged on Thursday after President Biden, when asked if he would support an Israeli strike on Iran's oil facilities, said: ''We're discussing that. I think that would be a little ... anyway.''\par \par \uc2 Economists are watching the development closely as they consider updating their forecasts.\par \par \uc2 ''So long as the conflict remains contained in the Middle East, the primary impact on the U.S. economy will likely be transmitted through energy prices,'' said \uc2 Michael Feroli\uc2 , chief U.S. economist at \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Analysts at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2  noted on Wednesday that Iranian oil constituted just 4 percent of global supplies but that disruptions to its output could have a significant impact on prices. That could be amplified if there are disruptions at the Strait of Hormuz, through which much of the region's oil and gas are shipped.\par \par \uc2 They suggested, however, that Saudi Arabia could ramp up production to make up for lost Iranian oil and said oil prices would most likely have to rise to $90 per barrel from the current price of about $75 for central banks to start worrying about inflation.\par \par \uc2 ''Crucially, it would also be how long this is sustained for it to really move the needle for central banks,'' David Oxley, chief climate and commodities economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said in a briefing. ''For that to happen we'd really have see a much bigger escalation in hostilities.''\par \par \uc2 Omair Sharif, founder of Inflation Insights, said that as a rule of thumb, a $10 rise in the cost of a barrel of oil translates to a 24-cent rise in the cost of a gallon of gasoline, which in turn would lift the monthly Consumer Price Index measure by 0.3 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 ''It could lead to second-order effects like higher airfares and higher diesel costs that boost the prices of some goods, but you'd need to see a sizable and persistent increase in oil for that to materialize,'' he added in an email.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the economic concern in the United States from the effects of Hurricane Helene.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 AccuWeather\uc2 , the damage and economic loss from the storm, which dumped more than 40 trillion gallons of rain, could total between $145 billion and $160 billion. That could hurt consumer spending in states such as Alabama, South Carolina, Georgia, Florida, North Carolina, Virginia and Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 There could also be a temporary slowdown in government revenues. The \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  has given businesses and individuals in hurricane-hit areas extra time to make tax payments.\par \par \uc2 While storms tend to have little impact on overall economic output, new cracks in the nation's supply chain -- a prospect raised when 45,000 workers at East and Gulf Coast ports went on strike on Tuesday -- would be a different matter. On Thursday, however, the union representing the workers, the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , agreed to suspend its strike after receiving an improved wage offer from port employers.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2  and Oliver Allen, economists at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said supply chains had enough flexibility such that a strike of just a few days would have a negligible impact on the American economy. Brief strikes by workers on the West Coast in 2002 and 2015 had no discernible impact.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration was closely monitoring the potential supply chain implications of the port strike, and officials have said they do not expect there to be an immediate impact on supplies of energy, food or medicines.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said this week that she stood with the longshoremen, who she argued deserve their ''fair share'' of the profits made by foreign-owned shipping companies.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  blamed the Biden administration for failing to help the two sides forge an agreement and said the dispute reflected the pressure that workers had been under from inflation. He warned that an extended strike would only make things worse.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a devastating event for the economy,'' Mr. Trump said in Wisconsin on Tuesday. ''It's also devastating for inflation because everything is going to cost more because of it.''\par \par \uc2 Jeanna Smialek and Danielle Kaye contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Jeanna Smialek and Danielle Kaye contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241004eka40003r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
