\par\par \uc2 STOCKS &AMP; BONDS\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Markets Meander to a Slight Rise as Oil Prices Taper\b0\par\par\uc2 By The Associated Press \par \uc2 697 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks edged higher in a quiet Wednesday, and Treasury yields rose following an encouraging update on the job market's strength.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 finished virtually unchanged, a day after sliding from its record on worries about a possible widening of the fighting in the Middle East. The Dow Jones industrial average edged up by 39 points, or 0.1 percent, and the Nasdaq composite added 0.1 percent.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices rose as the world waited to see how Israel would respond to Tuesday's missile attack from Iran, but they pared their gains as the day progressed. After briefly topping $76, the price for a barrel of Brent crude settled at $73.90, up 0.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 While Israel is not a major producer of oil, Iran is, and a worry is that a broadening war could affect neighboring countries that are also integral to the flow of crude. Helping to keep oil prices in check, meanwhile, are signals that supplies remain ample at the moment. The amount of crude in U.S. inventories increased last week, according to a \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  report.\par \par \uc2 In the bond market, Treasury yields rose after a report indicated hiring by U.S. employers outside the government may have been stronger last month than expected.\par \par \uc2 The report from ADP Research said private-sector employers accelerated their hiring in September. That could be an encouraging signal for the more comprehensive report on the U.S. job market that is due to arrive Friday from the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dominant question hanging over Wall Street has been whether the job market can keep holding up after the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  earlier kept interest rates at a two-decade high. The Fed was trying to press the brakes hard enough on the economy to stamp out high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Stocks are near records in large part on the belief that the U.S. economy will indeed continue to grow, now that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has shifted to cutting interest rates to give it more juice. The Fed last month lowered its main interest rate for the first time in more than four years and indicated more cuts will arrive through next year.\par \par \uc2 The yield on the 10-year Treasury rose to 3.78 percent from 3.73 percent late Tuesday. The two-year yield, which more closely follows expectations for what the Fed will do with overnight interest rates, rose to 3.63 percent from 3.61 percent.\par \par \uc2 Traders are ratcheting back their expectations for how much the Fed will cut rates at its next meeting in November. They're now mostly betting on a traditional cut of a quarter of a percentage point, according to data from \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Wall Street, \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2  jumped 5.3 percent for the biggest gain in the S&P 500. The casino owner said it approved a new program to deliver up to $500 million to shareholders by buying back more of its stock.\par \par \uc2 Ciena\uc2  climbed 7.4 percent after the networking company announced its own program to buy back up to $1 billion of its stock.\par \par \uc2 They helped offset an 11.8 percent tumble for \uc2 Humana\uc2  after the insurer warned a drop in its quality ratings for Medicare Advantage could mean a hit to its revenue in 2026. \uc2 Humana\uc2  said it believes there may be errors in the \uc2 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services\uc2 ' calculations, and it is trying to challenge the ratings.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  sank 6.8 percent even though the athletic giant reported stronger profit for the latest quarter than analysts expected. Its revenue fell short of forecasts, and the slump shows how much work the incoming chief executive, Elliott Hill, has in making the brand cool among customers. \uc2 Nike\uc2  also pulled its forecast for full-year financial results and postponed its investors day conference.\par \par \uc2 ConAgra Brands\uc2  fell 8.1 percent after the company behind Duncan Hines and Reddi-wip reported weaker profit than analysts expected. It said temporary manufacturing disruptions at its Hebrew National business during prime grilling season hurt its results.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020241003eka30004f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
