\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 As Strike Looms, Port Operators Ask Regulator to Force Dockworkers to Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Eavis \par \uc2 458 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The group that represents port terminal operators said the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  was refusing to negotiate a new contract before a Monday deadline.\par \par \uc2 Days ahead of a possible strike by longshoremen on the East and Gulf Coasts, port employers said on Thursday that they were asking a federal labor regulator to force the dockworkers' union to resume negotiating a new contract.\par \par \uc2 The United States Maritime Alliance, which is made up of port terminal operators, said it had filed an ''unfair labor practice'' complaint at the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  after, it said, the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  repeatedly refused to negotiate. The alliance said it wanted the labor board to rule that the union must negotiate with the employers.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Thursday, Jim McNamara, an I.L.A. spokesman, called the charge a ''publicity stunt'' that illustrated that the port employers were ''poor negotiating partners.''\par \par \uc2 Last week, the union said the two sides had ''communicated multiple times in recent weeks,'' and it contended that a stalemate existed because the Maritime Alliance was offering ''an unacceptable wage increase.''\par \par \uc2 A strike could begin on Tuesday, after the current labor contract expires on Monday. The I.L.A. broke off talks in June, contending that it had discovered that an employer was using labor-saving technology at the port in Mobile, Ala., that it claimed was unauthorized under the current contract.\par \par \uc2 A strike would close down nearly all activity at ports from Maine to Texas -- including at the Port of New York and New Jersey, the third busiest in the country. Analysts say even a short walkout could deal a blow to the economy. Fearing a strike, importers have been bringing in goods before next week and diverting some shipments to West Coast ports.\par \par \uc2 Officials in the Biden administration have said President Biden is not planning to force dockworkers back to work, which the 1947 Taft-Hartley Act authorizes him to do. But economists said Mr. Biden might well end up invoking the act if a strike dragged on.\par \par \uc2 Under the expiring contract, longshoremen earn $39 an hour. A person familiar with the negotiations said the union was asking for a $5-an-hour raise in each year of the new contract, which would last for six years. The person said employers were offering annual raises of $2.50 an hour.\par \par \uc2 The Maritime Alliance said Monday that it had been contacted by the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2 , a government agency that helps management and unions negotiate labor contracts.\par \par \uc2 Federal labor law says it is unlawful for a labor organization to refuse to negotiate on behalf of its members.\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240928ek9s0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
