\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 Nevada's Struggling Economy Is a Wild Card in the Election\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kurtis Lee \par \uc2 1,346 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2024\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state is among a handful that will decide the presidential contest, and workers have felt increased prices at the grocery store and gas station.\par \par \uc2 Sold-out shows along the Strip. Crews constructing a course for a major Formula 1 race. A record number of passengers at \uc2 Harry Reid International Airport\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For much of the past year, Las Vegas, the anchor of Nevada's economy, has watched in delight as visitors have flocked to town for conventions, football games and summer pool parties, further solidifying its rebound from the doldrums after the pandemic shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 But statewide, the economy is still burdened by high unemployment and higher costs of living -- twin pocketbook struggles that animate voters here in one of a handful of states expected to decide the November presidential election.\par \par \uc2 And about a quarter of Nevada voters in a New York Times/Siena College poll last month named the economy as their top issue. It was cited nearly twice as often as any other concern, comparable to findings in other swing states.\par \par \uc2 A topic with particular resonance among Nevada workers -- eliminating federal taxation on tips -- burst into the national discourse after former President Donald J. Trump told a crowd in Las Vegas that he intended to do away with the practice if elected. He was inspired, Mr. Trump has said, by a conversation with a waitress in the city.\par \par \uc2 His opponent, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , later endorsed the idea during a campaign stop in Las Vegas, but paired the proposal with a promise to raise the federal minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate in Nevada, where nearly a quarter of jobs are in leisure and hospitality, was 5.5 percent in August, the highest of any state. Statewide, gasoline prices hover near $4 a gallon, again among the nation's highest.\par \par \uc2 And in Las Vegas, where the unemployment rate was 6.7 percent in July, some residents feel squeezed by the forces of inflation and a surging housing market shaped, in part, by transplants from California and other states.\par \par \uc2 ''Everything has risen,'' said Ana Franco, who works delivering room service at the Bellagio. ''Food especially has gone way, way high.''\par \par \uc2 The employment outlook in Nevada has brightened since April 2020, when the pandemic shuttered casinos along the Strip for two months and unemployment spiked to a staggering 30 percent. Over the past year, there was a nearly 12 percent increase in construction jobs, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Leisure and hospitality employment rose 2.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 In June, the Las Vegas airport logged its third-busiest month in history, serving five million passengers. Last year, about 41 million people visited the city, an increase of around 5 percent from 2022, according to the \uc2 Las Vegas Convention and Visitors Authority\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''We've made significant progress facilitating Nevada's long-term economic recovery,'' Gov. \uc2 Joe Lombardo\uc2 , a Republican elected in 2022, said in a statement. But, he added, ''Nevadans are still suffering acutely from national inflation.''\par \par \uc2 Last month, Mr. Lombardo announced the creation of the Nevada Strategic Growth Initiative, a nonprofit focused on expanding and diversifying the state's economy. The board includes a former executive of a major resort and a former labor leader.\par \par \uc2 Over the past year, rising costs have been at the center of economic angst in the state.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 35,000 members of Culinary Workers Union Local 226 and Bartenders Union Local 165, a powerful base for Democrats, authorized a strike last fall, but last-minute negotiations with the three major resorts -- \uc2 Caesars Entertainment\uc2 , \uc2 MGM Resorts International\uc2  and \uc2 Wynn Resorts\uc2  -- averted a walkout. By the end of the five-year contract they ultimately secured, most workers will be paid at least $35 an hour.\par \par \uc2 This summer, a major issue among workers here has aligned with a catchphrase on the campaign trail: No taxes on tips.\par \par \uc2 Ted Pappageorge, the head of Local 226, which has endorsed Ms. Harris, said eliminating taxes on tips was a good policy only if combined with getting rid of the federal sub-minimum wage for tipped workers -- $2.13 an hour.\par \par \uc2 ''Ending taxes on tips for service and hospitality workers would significantly help millions of workers provide for their families, including in Nevada,'' Mr. Pappageorge said.\par \par \uc2 In interviews with workers and business owners in the Las Vegas area, high prices were a prominent concern. They were divided, however, about which candidate they trusted to improve the economy. (The Times/Siena poll, conducted Aug. 12-15, found an edge for Mr. Trump on that question, 56 percent to 41 percent, among likely Nevada voters.)\par \par \uc2 Ms. Franco, the Bellagio room service employee, said she was taxed per hour on the tips she made.\par \par \uc2 ''Sometimes we're getting taxed for something we don't make,'' said Ms. Franco, 51, a member of the Culinary Union who earns $17 an hour. ''If you have a slow day at work, you're still getting taxed.''\par \par \uc2 Nixing taxes on her tips, she said, would ''make a world of difference.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Franco said that she supported Ms. Harris and didn't blame the current administration for her state's economic struggles.\par \par \uc2 ''It happens all the time -- every few years we go through inflation,'' she said. ''I feel like it was going to happen anyway. We just didn't know when.''\par \par \uc2 Economic concerns extend to business owners like Javier Barajas, who opened a restaurant called Lindo Michoacan in 1990. He has expanded it to several locations while operating a Mexican and Italian fusion place as well -- and in recent months, both President Biden and Mr. Trump have visited his establishments.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Barajas said the overhead costs to run his businesses had tripled since last year, as the price of groceries have increased. The servers at his restaurants make $12 an hour, the state's minimum wage, plus tips, and hosts make $16 an hour because they do not earn tips.\par \par \uc2 ''It's very difficult,'' he said. ''It's getting harder and harder for small businesses to pay our bills.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Barajas, 65, said he planned to vote for Mr. Trump, partly because ''prices, everything, were just more stable five, six years ago.''\par \par \uc2 The search for financial stability is what brought Antonio Gonzalez-Petett to Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 A year ago, he moved with his wife and their young daughter from San Diego. For years, Mr. Gonzalez-Petett, 37, worked in the service industry. He knew he was unlikely to ever be able to own a home in Southern California, where median prices are close to $1 million.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after he arrived in Las Vegas, he began working at Lindo Michoacan as a bartender.\par \par \uc2 ''As an immigrant, owning a home is my dream -- it's the American dream,'' he said on a recent afternoon while on a break. In addition to earning $12 an hour, he makes $300 to $500 on tips each week. Only in recent months, he said, has earning enough to make a down payment on a home and cover the mortgage felt within reach.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Gonzalez-Petett sees the candidacy of Ms. Harris as historic, but he said inflation -- his main concern in this election -- took place under her leadership. He said he had not decided how to vote.\par \par \uc2 ''There is so much noise,'' he said. ''If you make below a certain amount of money in this country, then you're hurting financially.''\par \par \uc2 ''That,'' he added, ''needs to be corrected.''\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas has rebounded since the Covid shutdowns, but August's unemployment rate in the state, where nearly a quarter of jobs are in leisure and hospitality, was 5.5 percent. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MAGGIE SHANNON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B1); In Nevada, some residents feel squeezed by the forces of inflation and a surging housing market shaped, in part, by transplants from California and other states who came seeking homeownership. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY BRIDGET BENNETT FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; MARSHALL SCHEUTTLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (B4) This article appeared in print on page B1, B4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020240923ek9n0002u\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/10/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\